      "a": "<p>Model shape, substituting your own project. <em>General (~50 w):</em> \"It's a camera at the door that marks attendance by recognising faces, like your phone unlocking — but for a whole class in forty seconds.\" <em>Peer (~110 w):</em> \"Two stages: detect the face, then convert it into a 128-number signature and match it against the class list by distance, with a per-class threshold. The threshold is the whole game — loose and proxies get in, tight and real students get rejected.\" <em>Expert (~160 w):</em> \"RetinaFace-style detection plus a FaceNet-style embedding, triplet loss, fine-tuned on 210 identities; cosine distance, threshold 0.42 chosen on validation by F1. Baselines: pixel k-NN 71%, pre-trained 86%, ours 94%. 62% of residual errors are low light, 21% occlusion. No liveness detection, so a printed photo passes. Split by identity, to avoid leakage.\" <em>\"What would break this?\"</em> \"Any change in deployment conditions — a new camera angle, different lighting, or masked faces — because the threshold was tuned on one condition, and it would fail quietly rather than loudly, which is why low-confidence matches go to a review bucket instead of being marked absent.\" <strong>Key step:</strong> identical claims at all three rungs; only vocabulary, evidence density and the explicitness of limitations change.</p>",
